
  ARITHMETIC GEOMETRY THROUGH CAYLEY-RAPIDITY

======================================================================

  I. THE DEDEKIND ETA FUNCTION AND CAYLEY
  --------------------------------------------------

  eta(tau) = q^{1/24} * prod_{n=1}^{inf} (1-q^n)
  where q = e^{2*pi*i*tau}

  The 1/24 is the SAME 24 that appears everywhere:
  24 = |S_4| = |curvatures of S^2| = 2*3!+3*2!
  24 = SUM of the first 4 Hurwitz dimensions: 1+3+5+7+8
  Wait — that's 1+3+5+7 = 16. Actually: 24 = 4!

  But more deeply: 24 = (2^3)(3) = the group of
  SIGNED permutations of {1,2,3} = hyperoctahedral group W(B_3).

  eta(tau)^24 = Delta(tau) = q * prod(1-q^n)^24
  = sum tau(n) q^n  (Ramanujan's tau function)

  The discriminant form has weight 12 = phi(42)/1 = Cayley chromatic number
  It lives on SL_2(Z) = the FULL modular group.

  Euler product: Delta(s) has L-function
  L(Delta, s) = prod_p 1/(1 - tau(p)p^{-s} + p^{11-2s})

  The exponent 11 = 12-1 = weight-1.
  12 = phi(42). The discriminant IS the Cayley chromatic structure.


  II. BERNOULLI NUMBERS AND THE CAYLEY TRANSFORM
  --------------------------------------------------

  Bernoulli numbers (the architects of arithmetic):
    B_ 0 =               1
    B_ 1 =            -1/2
    B_ 2 =             1/6
    B_ 4 =           -1/30
    B_ 6 =            1/42
    B_ 8 =           -1/30
    B_10 =            5/66
    B_12 =       -691/2730
    B_14 =             7/6
    B_16 =       -3617/510
    B_18 =       43867/798

  The connection to zeta values:
  zeta(2k) = (-1)^{k+1} * (2*pi)^{2k} * B_{2k} / (2*(2k)!)

    zeta( 2) / pi^ 2 =             1/6
    zeta( 4) / pi^ 4 =            1/90
    zeta( 6) / pi^ 6 =           1/945
    zeta( 8) / pi^ 8 =          1/9450
    zeta(10) / pi^10 =         1/93555
    zeta(12) / pi^12 =   691/638512875
    zeta(14) / pi^14 =      2/18243225

  Bernoulli generates coth(x) = cosh/sinh (BOUNDED/UNBOUNDED)
  Euler numbers generate sech(x) = 1/cosh (the BRIDGE)
  The Cayley transform Q connects these:
  coth = Q(tanh) when restricted to the real line.

  Euler numbers (from sech(x)):
    E_ 0 =          1
    E_ 2 =         -1
    E_ 4 =          5
    E_ 6 =        -61
    E_ 8 =       1385
    E_10 =     -50521
    E_12 =    2702765

  |E_6| = 61 is prime. |E_8| = 1385 = 5 * 277.
  |E_10| = 50521 = 50521 (prime!).
  The Euler numbers are MUCH sparser than Bernoulli.
  They live on the BRIDGE (sech = bounded).


  III. DIRICHLET L-FUNCTIONS AND CUBOID POSITIONS
  --------------------------------------------------

  A Dirichlet character mod 42 = (mod 2) x (mod 3) x (mod 7)
  The group (Z/42Z)* has order phi(42) = 12.
  Characters factor: chi = chi_2 * chi_3 * chi_7

  Characters mod 2: trivial (both = 1)
  Characters mod 3: chi_0(trivial), chi_1(Legendre)
  Characters mod 7: chi_0, chi_1, chi_2, chi_3, chi_4, chi_5
  (6 characters = phi(7) = 6)

  Total: 1 * 2 * 6 = 12 = phi(42) characters. Correct.

  The quadratic character mod 7 (Legendre symbol):
    (1/7) = +1  [QR]
    (2/7) = +1  [QR]
    (3/7) = -1  [NR]
    (4/7) = +1  [QR]
    (5/7) = -1  [NR]
    (6/7) = -1  [NR]

  QR mod 7 = {1,2,4}. NR mod 7 = {3,5,6}.
  This IS the Paley tournament T_7!
  The quadratic character mod 7 = the adjacency matrix of T_7.

  L(1, chi_{-7}) = pi/sqrt(7) = 1.187410
  This is the class number formula: h(-7) = 1.
  Z[sqrt(-7)] has class number 1 = unique factorization.
  The FORBIDDEN prime 7 gives a PID!

  L(1, chi_{-3}) = pi/(3*sqrt(3)) = 0.604600
  h(-3) = 1. Z[omega] (Eisenstein integers) is a PID.

  h(-42) = 4. The Hurwitz discriminant -42 has class number 4.
  4 = 2^2 = the number of non-trivial ideal classes.
  These 4 classes correspond to the 4 ways to FAIL unique factorization
  in Z[sqrt(-42)]. The failure is measured by 4 = number of Cayley axes - 1.


  IV. THE RIEMANN ZETA AND CAYLEY-RAPIDITY
  --------------------------------------------------

  For prime p: Cayley address x_p = (p-1)/(p+1)
  Rapidity: rho_p = arctanh(x_p) = ln(p)/2

  In the Euler product: p^{-s} = e^{-2s * rho_p}
  So: zeta(s) = prod_p 1/(1 - e^{-2s*rho_p})

  This looks like a PARTITION FUNCTION!
  Each prime contributes a thermal factor 1/(1-e^{-beta*E_p})
  where beta = 2s and E_p = rho_p = ln(p)/2.

  The Riemann zeta function IS the GRAND CANONICAL PARTITION
  FUNCTION of a system where:
  - Each prime is a quantum oscillator
  - Energy of prime p = rho_p = half the logarithm
  - Inverse temperature = 2*Re(s)
  - Chemical potential = 2*Im(s)

  The critical strip 0 < Re(s) < 1:
  Re(s) = 1/2 is the critical line.
  In the thermal picture: beta_c = 2*(1/2) = 1.
  The critical temperature is beta = 1 (natural units).

  At beta = 1: each prime oscillator has occupation number
  <n_p> = 1/(e^{rho_p} - 1) = 1/(sqrt(p) - 1)

    p= 2: <n_p> = 1/(sqrt(2)-1) = 2.4142
    p= 3: <n_p> = 1/(sqrt(3)-1) = 1.3660
    p= 5: <n_p> = 1/(sqrt(5)-1) = 0.8090
    p= 7: <n_p> = 1/(sqrt(7)-1) = 0.6076
    p=11: <n_p> = 1/(sqrt(11)-1) = 0.4317
    p=13: <n_p> = 1/(sqrt(13)-1) = 0.3838
    p=17: <n_p> = 1/(sqrt(17)-1) = 0.3202
    p=19: <n_p> = 1/(sqrt(19)-1) = 0.2977
    p=23: <n_p> = 1/(sqrt(23)-1) = 0.2634
    p=29: <n_p> = 1/(sqrt(29)-1) = 0.2280
    p=31: <n_p> = 1/(sqrt(31)-1) = 0.2189
    p=37: <n_p> = 1/(sqrt(37)-1) = 0.1967
    p=41: <n_p> = 1/(sqrt(41)-1) = 0.1851
    p=43: <n_p> = 1/(sqrt(43)-1) = 0.1799

  The occupation drops as ~2/sqrt(p) for large p.
  The TOTAL occupation = sum <n_p> diverges (like sum 1/sqrt(p)).
  This divergence IS the pole of zeta(s) at s=1.


  V. MERTENS' THEOREMS IN RAPIDITY
  --------------------------------------------------

  Mertens I: sum_{p<=x} 2*rho_p / e^{2*rho_p} = ln(x) + O(1)
  In words: the rapidity-weighted sum of Boltzmann factors = energy.

  Mertens II: sum_{p<=x} e^{-2*rho_p} = ln(ln(x)) + M
  In words: the sum of Boltzmann factors = double-log + constant.
  M = 0.2615... = the MEISSEL-MERTENS constant.

  Meissel-Mertens constant M = 0.2614972128
  e^M = 1.2988733213
  e^(-M) = 0.7698980213

  Mertens III in Cayley address coordinates:
  prod_{p<=x} 2*x_p / (1+x_p) = e^{-gamma} / ln(x)
  where x_p = (p-1)/(p+1) is p's Cayley address.

  Accumulating Mertens product in Cayley coordinates:
