  VII. THE SATO-TATE DISTRIBUTION AND RAPIDITY
  --------------------------------------------------

  For a non-CM curve, a_p/(2*sqrt(p)) is distributed
  according to the Sato-Tate distribution:
  (2/pi) * sqrt(1-t^2) on [-1,1].

  Write a_p = 2*sqrt(p)*cos(theta_p).
  Then theta_p in [0,pi] is the SATO-TATE ANGLE.

  Connection to rapidity:
  The Sato-Tate angle theta_p is the argument of
  alpha_p = sqrt(p)*e^{i*theta_p} (the Frobenius eigenvalue).
  |alpha_p| = sqrt(p) = e^{rho_p} where rho_p = ln(p)/2.

  So alpha_p = e^{rho_p + i*theta_p}.
  This is a COMPLEX RAPIDITY: rho_p + i*theta_p
  The real part = log-size. The imaginary part = angle.
  The Frobenius IS a complex rapidity!

  Sato-Tate angles for 42a1:
